.Sh NAME
.Nm nvrm_client_open ,
.Nm nvrm_client_close ,
.Nm nvrm_client_errmsg ,
.Nm nvrm_client_stats_get ,
.Nm nvrm_vaspace_info_get ,
.Nm nvrm_va_reserve ,
.Nm nvrm_va_release ,
.Nm nvrm_mem_alloc ,
.Nm nvrm_mem_register_user ,
.Nm nvrm_mem_free ,
.Nm nvrm_mem_info_get ,
.Nm nvrm_mem_map_gpu ,
.Nm nvrm_mem_unmap_gpu ,
.Nm nvrm_mem_map_cpu ,
.Nm nvrm_mem_unmap_cpu ,
.Nm nvrm_mem_read ,
.Nm nvrm_mem_write ,
.Nm nvrm_mem_export_fd ,
.Nm nvrm_mem_import_fd
.Nd GPU memory and virtual address space over the NVIDIA Resource Manager
.Sh LIBRARY
libnvrm
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In nvrm/nvrm.h
.Ft int
.Fn nvrm_client_open "const nvrm_device *dev" "const nvrm_open_opts *opts" "nvrm_client **clp" "char *err" "size_t errlen"
.Ft void
.Fn nvrm_client_close "nvrm_client *cl"
.Ft const char *
.Fn nvrm_client_errmsg "const nvrm_client *cl"
.Ft int
.Fn nvrm_client_stats_get "nvrm_client *cl" "nvrm_client_stats *st"
.Ft int
.Fn nvrm_vaspace_info_get "nvrm_client *cl" "nvrm_vaspace_info *info"
.Ft int
.Fn nvrm_va_reserve "nvrm_client *cl" "const nvrm_va_params *p" "uint64_t *va"
.Ft int
.Fn nvrm_va_release "nvrm_client *cl" "uint64_t va"
.Ft int
.Fn nvrm_mem_alloc "nvrm_client *cl" "const nvrm_mem_params *p" "nvrm_mem **memp"
.Ft int
.Fn nvrm_mem_register_user "nvrm_client *cl" "void *addr" "uint64_t bytes" "nvrm_mem **memp"
.Ft void
.Fn nvrm_mem_free "nvrm_mem *mem"
.Ft int
.Fn nvrm_mem_info_get "const nvrm_mem *mem" "nvrm_mem_info *info"
.Ft int
.Fn nvrm_mem_map_gpu "nvrm_mem *mem" "const nvrm_gpu_map_params *p"
.Ft int
.Fn nvrm_mem_unmap_gpu "nvrm_mem *mem" "uint64_t va"
.Ft int
.Fn nvrm_mem_map_cpu "nvrm_mem *mem" "unsigned prot" "void **addr"
.Ft int
.Fn nvrm_mem_unmap_cpu "nvrm_mem *mem"
.Ft int
.Fn nvrm_mem_read "nvrm_mem *mem" "uint64_t offset" "void *buf" "size_t len"
.Ft int
.Fn nvrm_mem_write "nvrm_mem *mem" "uint64_t offset" "const void *buf" "size_t len"
.Ft int
.Fn nvrm_mem_export_fd "nvrm_mem *mem" "int *fd"
.Ft int
.Fn nvrm_mem_import_fd "nvrm_client *cl" "int fd" "nvrm_mem **memp"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These calls allocate GPU memory, give it GPU virtual addresses, map it for the
CPU and share it between processes, on a GPU found by
.Xr nvrm 3 .
They are the base the user-space graphics and compute drivers build on: a
Vulkan driver or a CUDA runtime gets its buffers and its GPU address space
here and submits work against them.
Every call works the same on the
.Dq rm
backend (the NVIDIA kernel module) and the
.Dq fake
backend (a software GPU for tests).
.Ss The client
.Fn nvrm_client_open
makes one client for one device
.Fa dev
from
.Fn nvrm_device_get :
a session of its own with the RM (its own control descriptor and RM client),
one GPU virtual address space, and every object made through it.
The client is independent of the context it was opened from and may outlive
it.
.Fn nvrm_client_close
frees every object, mapping and reservation still in the client.
On failure
.Pf * Fa clp
is
.Dv NULL
and
.Fa err ,
if not
.Dv NULL ,
holds one line saying why.
.Pp
.Fa opts
may be
.Dv NULL
for the defaults.
Its
.Va size
member is
.Li sizeof(nvrm_open_opts)
or 0;
.Va flags
is
.Dv NVRM_OPEN_ALLOW_MAP
or 0.
Without
.Dv NVRM_OPEN_ALLOW_MAP
the client can never map memory for the CPU and holds no descriptor that
could be mapped.
With it, the client opens
.Va map_slots
(0 means 16) spare mapping descriptors of this GPU's own device node
.Pf ( Pa /dev/nvidia Ns Ar N ,
with
.Ar N
reported by the driver) at once.
Every client opens
.Va export_slots
(0 means 4) spare export descriptors.
At most
.Dv NVRM_SLOTS_MAX
of each.
.Pp
Every structure with a
.Va size
member takes the size of the structure, or 0, which means the same; any other
value is refused with
.Dv NVRM_E_INVAL .
.Pp
.Fn nvrm_client_errmsg
returns one line about the most recent call on the client: the empty string
when it succeeded.
.Fn nvrm_client_stats_get
reports what the client holds and has done (objects, bytes, reservations, GPU
and CPU mappings, unused spare descriptors, and
.Va failed_releases ,
see
.Sx Releases the RM refuses ) .
.Ss Virtual address space
The library hands out the GPU virtual addresses; the RM maps at exactly the
addresses it is given.
.Fn nvrm_vaspace_info_get
reports the usable range (from
.Va base
to one below
.Va end )
and the bytes not reserved.
.Fn nvrm_va_reserve
reserves
.Va bytes
(a multiple of 4 KiB) aligned to
.Va alignment
(0 means 4 KiB; at least the page size of what will be mapped there), at
.Va fixed
when it is not 0, and returns the start in
.Pf * Fa va .
With
.Dv NVRM_VA_SPARSE
the addresses of the reservation that nothing is mapped at read as zero and
ignore writes instead of faulting.
.Fn nvrm_va_release
releases the reservation that starts at
.Fa va ;
it is refused with
.Dv NVRM_E_BUSY
while anything is mapped in it.
.Ss Memory objects
.Fn nvrm_mem_alloc
allocates
.Va bytes ,
rounded up to the page size, of
.Dv NVRM_MEM_VRAM
(the GPU's own memory; system memory on a GPU that has none) or
.Dv NVRM_MEM_SYSMEM
(system memory the GPU reaches, coherent with the CPU).
.Va page_size
is 4 KiB, 64 KiB, 2 MiB or 512 MiB, one the device lists in
.Va page_sizes ,
or 0, for which the library picks 64 KiB for video memory of at least 64 KiB
where the device lists it, and 4 KiB otherwise.
.Va alignment
is 0 or a power of two.
.Va flags
and
.Va reserved
must be 0.
.Fn nvrm_mem_register_user
makes an object of whole host pages of the caller's own address space; they
must stay mapped until the object is freed.
.Fn nvrm_mem_free
unmaps the object everywhere and frees it.
.Fn nvrm_mem_info_get
reports its kind, place
.Pq Dv NVRM_PLACE_VIDMEM or Dv NVRM_PLACE_SYSMEM ,
CPU attribute, size, page size and mappings.
.Ss GPU mappings
.Fn nvrm_mem_map_gpu
maps
.Va length
bytes (0 means to the end) of the object from
.Va offset
at the GPU address
.Va va .
The range must lie inside one reservation and overlap no other mapping;
.Va va ,
.Va offset
and
.Va length
are multiples of the object's page size, so reserve with an
.Va alignment
of at least that page size (64 KiB for the default video memory page).
.Dv NVRM_MAP_READ_ONLY
lets the GPU only read.
.Fn nvrm_mem_unmap_gpu
removes the object's mapping that starts at
.Fa va .
.Ss CPU mappings
.Fn nvrm_mem_map_cpu
maps the whole object for the CPU, with
.Fa prot
.Dv NVRM_CPU_READ ,
.Dv NVRM_CPU_WRITE
or both, and returns the address.
It needs a client opened with
.Dv NVRM_OPEN_ALLOW_MAP ,
and refuses registered user pages, which are mapped already.
The kernel module keeps one mapping per descriptor, so each CPU mapping holds
one mapping descriptor until it is unmapped.
.Fn nvrm_mem_unmap_cpu
removes it.
.Pp
The memory attribute of the mapping is
.Va cpu_attr
in
.Vt nvrm_mem_info ,
chosen by the rule of
.Xr nvrm 7 ,
.Sx Memory attributes :
.Dv NVRM_ATTR_CACHED
(any access),
.Dv NVRM_ATTR_WC
(write-combining) or
.Dv NVRM_ATTR_DEVICE
(Device memory, the arm64 Device-nGnRE type),
through which only aligned accesses of 1, 2, 4 or 8 bytes are safe: an
unaligned access, or a
.Xr memcpy 3
that makes one, can fault.
.Fn nvrm_mem_read
and
.Fn nvrm_mem_write
copy between the mapping and a buffer and obey the rule; code that reads or
writes the mapping itself must follow it.
.Ss Sharing
.Fn nvrm_mem_export_fd
returns a descriptor that names the object; it consumes one export
descriptor.
Pass it to another process
.Pq Xr unix 4 , Dv SCM_RIGHTS
or keep it, and close it when done.
.Fn nvrm_mem_import_fd
makes an object of the client from such a descriptor, on the same GPU; its
size, place and, for system memory, its CPU attribute are those the RM
reports for it.
Its page size is the smallest one the RM's answer allows: the RM reports a
huge page without saying 2 MiB or 512 MiB, so such an import has 2 MiB, and a
mapping that does not fit a 512 MiB page is then refused by the RM
.Pq Dv NVRM_E_RM .
An import whose page size the RM does not report at all is refused.
Registered user pages cannot be exported.
.Ss Capability mode
.Fn nvrm_client_open
opens and limits every descriptor the client needs: its session descriptor
keeps only the RM escapes it uses (and the CPU mapping ones only with
.Dv NVRM_OPEN_ALLOW_MAP ) ,
a mapping spare only the right to be mapped, an export spare only the right
to be read.
After
.Xr cap_enter 2
the client keeps working, with the spares as the budget: each CPU mapping or
export uses one for good, and when they are gone the call fails with
.Dv NVRM_E_NOSLOT
because nothing can be opened in capability mode.
Outside capability mode a missing spare is simply opened.
.Ss Releases the RM refuses
An unmap or a free the RM refuses counts in
.Va failed_releases
and leaves its reason in
.Fn nvrm_client_errmsg .
.Fn nvrm_mem_unmap_gpu ,
.Fn nvrm_mem_unmap_cpu
and
.Fn nvrm_va_release
also return
.Dv NVRM_E_RM
(or
.Dv NVRM_E_IO
when the system call itself failed);
.Fn nvrm_mem_free
returns nothing, and a refusal inside
.Fn nvrm_client_close
cannot be seen, because the client is gone after it.
What it held stays with the RM client until the client closes, and its
addresses are never handed out again:
a refused
.Fn nvrm_mem_unmap_gpu
or
.Fn nvrm_va_release
leaves the mapping or the reservation in place, and the call can be repeated;
a mapping whose unmap is refused while its object is freed keeps its
addresses and its reservation busy;
a refused
.Fn nvrm_mem_unmap_cpu
still removes the CPU side.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The calls that return
.Vt int
return 0 on success, or one of the codes below;
.Fn nvrm_strerror
and
.Fn nvrm_error_id
of
.Xr nvrm 3
turn a code into text and a stable identifier, and
.Fn nvrm_client_errmsg
says more.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width ".Ev NVRM_FAKE_HOST"
.It Ev NVRM_FAKE_HOST
For the fake backend:
.Dq arm64
or
.Dq amd64 ,
the host the memory attribute rule is applied for (default: the real one).
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Allocate 1 MiB of video memory, give it a GPU address, and write it from the
CPU:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nvrm_open_opts o = { 0, NVRM_OPEN_ALLOW_MAP, 0, 0 };
nvrm_va_params vp = { 0, 0, 1 << 20, 1 << 16, 0 };	/* 64 KiB aligned */
nvrm_mem_params mp = { 0, NVRM_MEM_VRAM, 0, 0, 1 << 20, 0, 0 };
nvrm_gpu_map_params gp = { 0 };
nvrm_client *cl;
nvrm_mem *m;
uint64_t va;
uint32_t v = 1;
void *p;
char err[160];

if (nvrm_client_open(nvrm_device_get(ctx, 0), &o, &cl, err,
    sizeof(err)) != 0)
	errx(1, "%s", err);
if (nvrm_va_reserve(cl, &vp, &va) != 0 ||
    nvrm_mem_alloc(cl, &mp, &m) != 0)
	errx(1, "%s", nvrm_client_errmsg(cl));
gp.va = va;
if (nvrm_mem_map_gpu(m, &gp) != 0 ||
    nvrm_mem_map_cpu(m, NVRM_CPU_READ | NVRM_CPU_WRITE, &p) != 0 ||
    nvrm_mem_write(m, 0, &v, sizeof(v)) != 0)
	errx(1, "%s", nvrm_client_errmsg(cl));
nvrm_client_close(cl);
.Ed
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er NVRM_E_INVAL
An argument breaks a rule above: a wrong
.Va size ,
an unknown flag, a size or address not a multiple of the page size, no
reservation or mapping at the address given, a registered object where it is
not allowed.
.It Bq Er NVRM_E_RANGE
No free VA range of that size or at that address; a GPU mapping outside one
reservation or over another mapping; an offset or access outside the object.
.It Bq Er NVRM_E_BUSY
The reservation still has mappings, or the object is mapped already.
.It Bq Er NVRM_E_DENIED
The client was not opened with
.Dv NVRM_OPEN_ALLOW_MAP ,
the CPU mapping does not allow the access, or this process did not open the
client: in a
.Xr fork 2
child a call that would create or act on RM objects is refused, while
releases succeed and drop only the child's records.
The getters,
.Fn nvrm_mem_read
and
.Fn nvrm_mem_write
are not checked, and CPU mappings the child inherited still reach the
memory: unmap them before
.Xr fork 2 ,
or mark them with
.Xr minherit 2 ,
to keep a child out.
.It Bq Er NVRM_E_NOSLOT
No spare descriptor is left in capability mode.
.It Bq Er NVRM_E_NOTSUP
The backend has no memory support; or the GPU has no device node, so
.Dv NVRM_OPEN_ALLOW_MAP
cannot be honoured (open without it to use GPU mappings only); or the RM
cannot express what was asked, or allocated with a page size the library
does not know.
.It Bq Er NVRM_E_RM
The RM refused a call; the message names the call and the RM status.
.It Bq Er NVRM_E_IO
A system call to the driver failed.
.It Bq Er NVRM_E_OPEN
A device node could not be opened.
.It Bq Er NVRM_E_CAPSICUM
A descriptor could not be limited.
.It Bq Er NVRM_E_NOMEM
Out of memory.
From
.Fn nvrm_va_release
it means the reservation is released, but its addresses are kept out of use
for the life of the client: do not retry.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr cap_enter 2 ,
.Xr nvrm 3 ,
.Xr capsicum 4 ,
.Xr nvrm 7
.Sh STABILITY
This interface is provisional.
The symbol version
.Li NVRM_1.1
and the structures behind it are frozen at the first package release and not
before.
.Sh HISTORY
These calls first appeared with API minor 1 of the library.
.Sh CAVEATS
A client is not safe for concurrent use: use one thread per client, or a lock
of your own.
The fake backend keeps process-wide test state: use it from one thread.
